WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.940
So far a matrix was a table of numbers

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.420
acting on a column of numbers.

3
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.700
Today, watch one act on the whole plane.

4
00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.667
Every grid line stays straight

5
00:00:14.751 --> 00:00:17.114
parallel lines stay parallel

6
00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:18.718
and evenly spaced

7
00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.250
and the origin does not move.

8
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.594
Now a very different map: the derivative.

9
00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:28.906
It sends three minus two t plus t

10
00:00:29.003 --> 00:00:32.120
squared to minus two plus two t.

11
00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.779
Is the derivative a matrix too?

12
00:00:34.854 --> 00:00:36.791
Pause and think about what

13
00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:38.430
that would even mean.

14
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.028
First, widen the idea of a vector.

15
00:00:45.028 --> 00:00:48.933
A vector is anything you can add and scale

16
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:51.330
following the usual rules.

17
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.953
Functions can be added and scaled.

18
00:00:54.953 --> 00:00:58.803
Let the arrow e one stand for cosine t

19
00:00:58.803 --> 00:01:00.920
and e two for sine t.

20
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.714
Two cosine t plus five sine

21
00:01:03.805 --> 00:01:07.335
t is then one arrow, built tip to tail

22
00:01:07.335 --> 00:01:10.051
exactly like a column vector.

23
00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:13.310
This plane is a plane of functions.

24
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.347
The polynomials of degree at most two

25
00:01:17.347 --> 00:01:20.453
called P two, form a space too.

26
00:01:20.453 --> 00:01:23.655
But degree exactly two does not

27
00:01:23.655 --> 00:01:27.827
t squared plus one minus t squared is one

28
00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:29.380
of degree zero.

29
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.291
A basis gives coordinates.

30
00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:35.159
In the basis one, t, t squared

31
00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:38.566
our polynomial has coordinates three

32
00:01:38.566 --> 00:01:40.000
minus two, one.

33
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.650
Now draw the polynomial itself, as a curve.

34
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:48.550
Centre the basis at t equals one: one

35
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.350
t minus one, and t minus one squared.

36
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.350
Now the coordinates are two, zero, one.

37
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.957
The new coordinates tell you something.

38
00:01:59.957 --> 00:02:03.213
The two is p of one, the lowest value.

39
00:02:03.297 --> 00:02:05.468
The zero says the slope at

40
00:02:05.551 --> 00:02:07.305
t equals one is zero

41
00:02:07.305 --> 00:02:10.060
this is the bottom of the curve.

42
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.705
The vector did not change

43
00:02:12.705 --> 00:02:14.647
only its description.

44
00:02:14.647 --> 00:02:18.090
A good basis makes a property visible.

45
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.358
Changing coordinates is a matrix.

46
00:02:21.358 --> 00:02:23.557
Put the new basis vectors

47
00:02:23.557 --> 00:02:25.837
written in old coordinates

48
00:02:25.837 --> 00:02:27.710
into the columns of B.

49
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.515
So to get the new coordinates

50
00:02:31.515 --> 00:02:34.740
multiply the old ones by B?

51
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.540
That gives six, minus four, one.

52
00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.162
Rebuild the polynomial from

53
00:02:41.246 --> 00:02:43.855
those coordinates, and draw it.

54
00:02:43.855 --> 00:02:47.305
It is eleven minus six t plus t squared

55
00:02:47.305 --> 00:02:50.671
the same shape, moved two to the right.

56
00:02:50.671 --> 00:02:52.690
A different polynomial.

57
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.253
B goes from new to old

58
00:02:55.253 --> 00:02:58.621
old coordinates equal B times new.

59
00:02:58.621 --> 00:03:01.604
So old to new needs B inverse

60
00:03:01.604 --> 00:03:04.298
which gives two, zero, one

61
00:03:04.298 --> 00:03:06.800
and the curve comes back.

62
00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.625
Now the maps.

63
00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:11.785
A linear transformation respects

64
00:03:11.881 --> 00:03:13.700
sums and multiples.

65
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.116
Take two arrows, u and v, and their sum

66
00:03:18.116 --> 00:03:21.674
the diagonal of the parallelogram.

67
00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:23.402
Apply the shear

68
00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:27.874
the parallelogram is still a parallelogram

69
00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:31.330
so T of u plus v is T u plus T v.

70
00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.017
Scaling works the same way

71
00:03:34.017 --> 00:03:37.480
twice an arrow goes to twice its image.

72
00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.897
Adding a fixed vector is not linear

73
00:03:40.897 --> 00:03:42.685
it moves the origin.

74
00:03:42.685 --> 00:03:46.178
Squaring is not linear either: double p

75
00:03:46.178 --> 00:03:49.670
and p squared becomes four times as big.

76
00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.409
A linear map is known once

77
00:03:52.492 --> 00:03:54.401
you know it on a basis

78
00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:57.556
because every vector is a combination

79
00:03:57.639 --> 00:03:59.050
of basis vectors.

80
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.766
So record exactly that.

81
00:04:01.766 --> 00:04:05.160
The shear sends e one to itself

82
00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.526
and e two to one, one.

83
00:04:07.526 --> 00:04:11.949
Those two images are the two columns of N

84
00:04:11.949 --> 00:04:14.520
column j holds T of v j.

85
00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:17.636
Now the derivative on P two.

86
00:04:17.636 --> 00:04:20.896
D of one is zero, D of t is one

87
00:04:20.896 --> 00:04:23.366
D of t squared is two t.

88
00:04:23.465 --> 00:04:25.934
Their coordinates are the

89
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:27.910
three columns of D.

90
00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.865
Multiply D by three, minus two, one.

91
00:04:31.865 --> 00:04:34.868
You get minus two, two, zero

92
00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.972
the line minus two plus two t

93
00:04:37.972 --> 00:04:41.577
which crosses zero at t equals one

94
00:04:41.577 --> 00:04:44.380
the bottom of the parabola.

95
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.021
Differentiation has become

96
00:04:47.109 --> 00:04:49.048
matrix multiplication.

97
00:04:49.136 --> 00:04:51.956
And three derivatives kill every

98
00:04:52.044 --> 00:04:54.248
polynomial of degree two

99
00:04:54.248 --> 00:04:56.010
so D cubed is zero.

100
00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.334
Back to the plane of functions

101
00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:02.786
with cosine t and sine t as the basis.

102
00:05:02.874 --> 00:05:05.264
The derivative sends cosine

103
00:05:05.353 --> 00:05:09.070
t to minus sine t, and sine t to cosine t.

104
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.392
Write those as columns: zero, minus one

105
00:05:13.392 --> 00:05:14.882
and one, zero.

106
00:05:14.882 --> 00:05:17.265
As a warp of the plane

107
00:05:17.265 --> 00:05:20.740
that is a quarter turn, clockwise.

108
00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.673
Watch two cosine t plus five

109
00:05:23.766 --> 00:05:25.426
sine t ride along.

110
00:05:25.426 --> 00:05:29.486
It lands on five cosine t minus two sine t

111
00:05:29.486 --> 00:05:32.530
which is exactly its derivative.

112
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.596
Every arrow turns

113
00:05:34.596 --> 00:05:38.217
so no real arrow keeps its direction

114
00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:41.839
the eigenvalues are plus and minus i.

115
00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:45.461
That is why e to the i t appeared for

116
00:05:45.556 --> 00:05:48.320
the spring in Lecture twenty.

117
00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.937
So: a linear map keeps sums and multiples

118
00:05:52.937 --> 00:05:57.167
a basis gives every vector its coordinates

119
00:05:57.167 --> 00:06:01.300
and column j of the matrix holds T of v j.

120
00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.543
Next, one map seen in two different bases

121
00:06:05.543 --> 00:06:09.250
two matrices, and what they must share.
